%[text] %[text:anchor:T_157DFE85] # Support Document for Arduino
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:80ed)
%[text] This script will guide you through the process of installing the support packages for your Arduino hardware and help you set up your board.
%%
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_29600648] ## Hardware Support Packages
%[text] MATLAB and Simulink support Arduino Hardware through two dedicated support packages:
%[text] 1. [MATLAB Support Package for Arduino Hardware](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/47522-matlab-support-package-for-arduino-hardware)
%[text] 2. [Simulink Support Package for Arduino Hardware](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/40312-simulink-support-package-for-arduino-hardware) \
%[text] Depending on your usage, you might only rely on one or the other support package. In this Introduction to Engineering module, we designed the learning experience utilizing both MATLAB and Simulink, so we require the installation of both support packages.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A78E4B27] ### Supported Hardware
%[text] Arduino is an open-source electronic platform that provides a software and hardware environment that allows users to create exciting digital devices and interactive objects. Traditionally, Arduinos are programmed using the [Arduino IDE](https://www.arduino.cc/en/software) to push C/C++ code to the board. MATLAB and Simulink provide an environment allowing the control of the same hardware using MATLAB function and Simulink Blockset. The support packages support the following Arduino boards:
%[text] 
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Classic boards** | **Nano boards** | **MKR boards** | **Other boards** | **Shield 7 kits** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | <p>Arduino Uno</p><p>Arduino Mega</p><p>Arduino Leonardo</p><p>Arduino Due</p><p>Arduino Micro</p><p>Arduino Mega-ADK</p> | <p>Arduino Nano 3.0</p><p>**Arduino Nano 33 IoT**</p> | <p>Arduino MKR 1000</p><p>**Arduino MKR WiFi**</p><p>Arduino MKR Zero</p> | <p>ESP8266 Module</p><p>ESP32 WROOM DevKit C</p><p>ESP32 WROOM DevKit V1</p><p>ESP32-WROVER boards</p> | <p>Arduino Ethernet Shield</p><p>Arduino WiFi Shield</p><p>Arduino MKR Motor</p><p>Arduino Nano Motor</p><p>Arduino Robot</p><p>Arduino Engineering Kit</p> |
%[text:table]
%[text] \* Bold font is used for board with native WiFi support
%[text] The support package can be used for various applications, including robotics, sensor, automation, and data acquisition. With these packages, users can easily create and simulate models in Simulink that interface with the board's I/O pins, sensors, actuators, motors, and other peripherals. Additionally, the package enables users to analyze and visualize data from the boards in MATLAB, making it easier to debug and fine-tune their designs.
%[text] The scripts provided in this module assume that the user has installed the two support packages. Follow the instructions below to check your installations.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B846371E] ### MATLAB Support Package
%[text] MATLAB® Support Package for Arduino® Hardware enables you to use MATLAB to communicate with an Arduino board interactively.
%[text] For instance, you can read and write sensor data through the Arduino board and immediately see the results in MATLAB without compiling any code. Further, you can utilize the built-in math, engineering, and plotting functions of MATLAB to analyze and visualize data collected from your Arduino.
%[text] You can also create and distribute compiled standalone MATLAB applications that run MATLAB programs on systems to interface Arduino hardware over Serial without an installed version of MATLAB.
  %[control:button:4070]{"position":[1,2]}
myAddons = matlab.addons.installedAddons().Name;
if any(contains(myAddons,"MATLAB Support Package for Arduino Hardware"))
    disp("MATLAB Support Package for Arduino Hardware is installed and enabled")
    disp("No other action required.")
else
    if ~exist("isTest","var") % if this line of code is reach outside running test (view SoftwareTests/SmokeTests.m)
        open("arduinoio.mlpkginstall")
    end
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C2C9BD18] ### Simulink Support Package
%[text] Simulink® Support Package for Arduino® hardware will empower you with the latest Model-Based Design technology to create embedded systems on Arduino, from simulation to implementation.
  %[control:button:1c2a]{"position":[1,2]}
if any(contains(myAddons,"Simulink Support Package for Arduino Hardware"))
    disp("Simulink Support Package for Arduino Hardware is installed and enabled")
    disp("No other action required.")
else
    if ~exist("isTest","var") % if this line of code is reach outside running test (view SoftwareTests/SmokeTests.m)
        open("arduino.mlpkginstall")
    end
end
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E4E74495] ## Internet of Things (IoT) using ThingSpeak
%[text] The Internet of Things (IoT) refers to the network of physical devices, vehicles, appliances, and other objects embedded with sensors, software, and connectivity, allowing them to collect and exchange data. These interconnected devices can communicate and interact with each other, enabling automation and data-driven decision-making. ThingSpeak is an IoT platform that facilitates data collection, analysis, and visualization from IoT devices, including Arduino hardware. It provides an easy-to-use interface for users to create and manage IoT applications.
%[text] To create your ThingSpeak account and check if your license covers ThingSpeak, visit the website:
  %[control:button:7439]{"position":[1,2]}
web("https://thingspeak.com/")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F8F020E4] ## 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BE805DEB] ## Setting your Hardware to work with Simulink
%[text] With both Arduino Support packages installed, you should be able to configure your Arduino hardware using the Arduino Hardware Setup application.
 if false %[control:statebutton:3edc]{"position":[5,10]}
    arduinosetup
 end
%%
